(98, 'How to command RSYNC with include and exclude', 'How to command rsync with include and exclude option\nExample:\n\nrsync -av --include=\"ej.*\" --exclude=\"*.*\" /home/manager/main/backup/????25 .\n\nThis command execute to copy all ej file including the directory with differrent backup date directory, So I expect that i can only see \"ej.001\" when i open different backup date directory.', '2025-05-26 22:38:48', '2025-05-26 22:38:48', NULL),
(99, ' 🔍How to Locate Files Efficiently in Red Hat Linux ', '🧭 Overview\nIn Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL), locating files is an essential skill for system admins, developers, and power users alike. Whether you\'re debugging a misconfigured service or just hunting down a forgotten log file, RHEL provides a range of tools to streamline your file-finding workflow.\n🛠️ Common Tools for Finding Files\n- find: Powerful and flexible, lets you search by name, size, modified time, and more.\nfind /path/to/search -name \"filename.txt\"\n- locate: Uses a prebuilt index for lightning-fast lookups. Requires mlocate package and regular updates.\nlocate filename.txt\n- which & whereis: Useful for finding binary locations and related files.\nwhich python\nwhereis ssh\n- grep + find combo: For locating files based on content.\nfind /var/log -type f -exec grep -i \"error\" {} \\;\n\n\n🚀 Real-World Use Cases\n- Tracking config files: Search /etc for .conf files.\nfind /etc -type f -name \"*.conf\"\n- Cleaning up disk space: Find large or old files in user directories.\nfind /home -type f -size +500M\nfind /home -type f -mtime +30\n- System monitoring: Locate logs or temp files in /var.\nfind /var -type f -name \"*.log\"\n\n\n🔐 Pro Tips\n- Use wildcards (*, ?) and filters (-type, -size, -mtime) for precision.\n- Combine find with xargs for batch operations.\n- Redirect output to a file for record-keeping:\nfind /opt -name \"*.sh\" > scripts_list.txt', '2025-07-22 19:30:42', '2025-07-22 19:30:42', NULL);
